messages:
Jordan P: Quick heads-up — onboarding sampleapp running with default settings just caused a noisy incident in my laptop runs: multiple 429s, then retries gumming up the event loop. Repro attached.

Jordan P: Steps: start sampleapp with `--concurrency 200` (defaults), run 20 clients that paginate and call .embed() + .chat(). Observed spike of concurrent inflight sequences and then steady 429s.

Sofia M: ugh. Is this the same token refresh stampede we saw last quarter or something different?

Jordan P: Similar root cause but different surface: even after token refresh fix in JS, our retry path in the paginator immediately requeues without honoring the per-client concurrency cap, so retries multiply.

Ilya R: Can you paste or gist the retry loop? Might be the retry wrapper re-acquires semaphore incorrectly.

Jordan P: snippet:
```js
async function pageFetch(cursor) {
  const slot = await concurrency.acquire();
  try {
    const t = await auth.getToken(); // single-flight in place
    return await client.list({ cursor, token: t });
  } catch (e) {
    if (isRetryable(e)) {
      // currently we immediately retry
      return await pageFetch(cursor);
    }
    throw e;
  } finally {
    slot.release();
  }
}
```

Sam T: That's the problem — recursive retry bypasses outer callers' expectations and can create N extra stack frames waiting for tokens. We should enqueue retries into the same concurrency pool instead of recursion.

Sofia M: Also, onboarding runs often use the CLI where users crank concurrency to speed tests. We should make safer defaults in sampleapp: lower default concurrency (16), enable `--burst-safe` by default for local runs, and expose a `--unsafe-thruput` flag for power users.

Ilya R: SDK parity item: JS already has single-flight token refresh, but Python still calls refresh per request in our examples. I'll open a parity PR to add the refresh-promise pattern and Config.max_sequences with default 16.

Sam T: Telemetry idea: emit a metric sdk.client.refresh.attempts and sdk.client.refresh.concurrent_max so we can detect stampedes in hosted and onboard runs. Also add a sampleapp health-check that warns when `concurrency > 64` and not in burst-safe mode.

Jordan P: For retries we should implement an explicit retry-queue that acquires a concurrency slot before attempting the retry and uses exp backoff + jitter with capped retries. Pseudocode:
```js
async function retryable(fn) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (true) {
    await concurrency.acquire();
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) {
      attempt++;
      if (!isRetryable(e) || attempt > 5) throw e;
    } finally { concurrency.release(); }
    await sleep(expBackoff(attempt) + jitter());
  }
}
```

Sofia M: Good. Docs change: update onboarding README with a "Burst safety" section explaining defaults, how to opt-in/out, and recommended limits for Hosted vs Dedicated users (hosted: keep concurrency <= 20 per CLI session; dedicated: document how to raise safely).

Ilya R: Tests: I'll add a flakey harness that simulates 1% token expiry and a burst of 500 requests to assert no more than 1 concurrent refresh and that memory doesn't exceed a threshold.

DevX-Bot: Created ticket DE-3201: "onboarding: burst-safe sampleapp defaults, retry queue, SDK parity for refresh" and assigned to Ilya R, Sam T, Sofia M.

Sam T: Action items recap:
- Ilya: PR for Python SDK single-flight + config.max_sequences (default 16)
- Sam: implement retry-queue in sampleapp + CLI health-check
- Sofia: docs + onboarding README updates + recommended hosted/dedicated numbers
- Jordan: attach repro scripts and results to DE-3201

Jordan P: Thanks team — I'll drop the flamegraph and the small repro harness in the ticket. Will also add a short note in the quickstart to prevent users from running the unsafe default. :thumbsup:
